WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.885
Picture the last day of the month.

2
00:00:04.885 --> 00:00:11.503
Card payments pour into Kafka, a program that keeps logs of messages and lets other programs read them.

3
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.734
The ledger, the program that keeps each account's balance, reads them.

4
00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:20.041
It handles 8 payments a second, and 30 arrive.

5
00:00:20.041 --> 00:00:23.133
After an hour, it's 79,200 payments behind.

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.494
So the team starts two more copies.

7
00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:29.004
A few seconds later, the logs show payments added twice.

8
00:00:29.004 --> 00:00:31.010
Where did the doubles come from?

9
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.201
Hold that question.

10
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:34.367
First, how copies share the work.

11
00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:39.606
A restaurant gives each table to one waiter, and a waiter looks after several tables.

12
00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:42.935
Nobody gets three desserts, and courses come in order.

13
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.385
Kafka does the same.

14
00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:50.617
The three copies form a consumer group, and the group gives each partition to exactly one member.

15
00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.958
When members join or leave, partitions change owner.

16
00:00:53.958 --> 00:00:55.628
That's called a rebalance.

17
00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:57.159
The partition moves.

18
00:00:57.159 --> 00:00:59.345
But what happens to its bookmark?

19
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.183
Take partition 3, before the move.

20
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:06.723
c1 owns it, and has processed everything before offset 340.

21
00:01:06.723 --> 00:01:12.109
It commits after every batch of 100, so the group's bookmark says 300.

22
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.598
Now c2 joins, and the group gives partition 3 to c2.

23
00:01:16.598 --> 00:01:18.251
Where does c2 start?

24
00:01:18.251 --> 00:01:19.573
Pause and guess.

25
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.741
At 300, where the bookmark says.

26
00:01:25.741 --> 00:01:27.786
c2 knows nothing else.

27
00:01:27.786 --> 00:01:31.597
So it processes 300 to 339 a second time.

28
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:38.515
And if c1 is still busy with that batch when c2 starts, both apply the same payments at the same moment.

29
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:41.647
The lesson's lab takes the handover away.

30
00:01:41.647 --> 00:01:46.763
Three consumers, 120 payments in the topic, and the group processes 200.

31
00:01:46.763 --> 00:01:50.529
Eighty of them twice, just from adding two consumers.

32
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:52.071
Back to the restaurant.

33
00:01:52.071 --> 00:01:56.084
A good waiter doesn't walk away from a table in the middle of a meal.

34
00:01:56.084 --> 00:02:00.097
Before the table moves, they tell the next waiter what's been served.

35
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.137
Let's map it.

36
00:02:01.137 --> 00:02:07.127
The table is the partition, the waiter is the consumer, and the note is the committed offset.

37
00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:10.155
The moment just before leaving is the handover.

38
00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:12.843
Kafka gives the consumer that moment.

39
00:02:12.843 --> 00:02:21.184
You register a ConsumerRebalanceListener: three functions the consumer calls, from inside poll, when its partitions change.

40
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.371
onPartitionsRevoked: these are leaving, and they're still yours.

41
00:02:25.371 --> 00:02:29.093
Finish or drop the current work, and commit how far you got.

42
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.320
onPartitionsAssigned: these are new to you, and you start at their committed offsets.

43
00:02:34.533 --> 00:02:38.763
Replay the move with that commit, and c2 starts at 340.

44
00:02:38.763 --> 00:02:40.840
Nothing is processed twice.

45
00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:48.356
And onPartitionsLost: the group has already given these to someone else, usually because this member was thrown out.

46
00:02:48.356 --> 00:02:49.176
Don't commit.

47
00:02:49.176 --> 00:02:51.891
That bookmark isn't yours to move any more.

48
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.311
Why is this safe?

49
00:02:53.311 --> 00:02:54.806
One rule about order.

50
00:02:54.806 --> 00:03:00.931
The group never gives a partition to its new owner until the old owner has revoked it.

51
00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:04.991
So c1's commit always lands before c2 reads the bookmark.

52
00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:10.708
Now a tempting idea: commit in onPartitionsLost too, just to be safe.

53
00:03:10.708 --> 00:03:12.154
Follow it through.

54
00:03:12.154 --> 00:03:15.126
c1 was thrown out while stuck at 300.

55
00:03:15.126 --> 00:03:20.105
c2 already owns partition 3 and has moved the bookmark to 360.

56
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:25.940
Then c1's late commit drags it back to 300, and the next owner repeats sixty payments.

57
00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:32.367
It's a waiter who was sent home, phoning in to change the bill of a table someone else now serves.

58
00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:33.441
One more note.

59
00:03:33.441 --> 00:03:38.877
A member that dies without revoking can't commit anything, so repeats can still happen.

60
00:03:38.877 --> 00:03:43.001
That's why the ledger still checks each payment's idempotency key.

61
00:03:43.001 --> 00:03:45.500
But a planned rebalance repeats nothing.

62
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.387
In Java and Kotlin, you pass the listener to subscribe.

63
00:03:49.387 --> 00:03:54.951
Commit in onPartitionsRevoked, and in onPartitionsLost, just forget the partitions.

64
00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:57.819
So where did the doubles come from?

65
00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:00.217
Partitions that moved without a commit.

66
00:04:00.217 --> 00:04:02.737
The new owner started at an old bookmark.

67
00:04:02.737 --> 00:04:06.488
A partition that moves without a commit gets processed twice.

68
00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:10.421
One quick check, with numbers made up for it.

69
00:04:10.421 --> 00:04:16.520
c1 owns partition 4, has processed everything before 520, and last committed 500.

70
00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:19.080
It commits in onPartitionsRevoked.

71
00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.414
Where does the new owner start?

72
00:04:21.414 --> 00:04:22.619
Pause and think.

73
00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:26.386
At 520.

74
00:04:26.386 --> 00:04:32.771
The handover commit moves the bookmark to the next record to read, so nothing repeats.
